course: dictionary
lesson: 9657
title: Polite words for bad news
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Suboptimal» is a long word, but its parts help. Sub means under, and optimal means the best possible, so suboptimal means under the best. It is not as good as it could be.
explain: In plain words, suboptimal means poor or bad. People often use it to sound polite. «"Suboptimal" means bad or poor» is the simple meaning, but grown-ups may pick it so they do not have to say bad out loud.
explain: Watch for spin. «And some words spin a failure to sound better, such as calling a bad result "suboptimal".» Ask yourself: is the writer being polite, being funny, or hiding how bad it really is? Then swap in "bad" or "poor" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: «Suboptimal» means not as good as it could be; in plain words, poor or bad.
fact: In maths or computing, «suboptimal» means not the best possible solution.
fact: It is jargon, used in business, science and polite criticism, so people can avoid saying "bad".
fact: Sometimes it is used as a funny understatement, like saying a disaster was "a bit suboptimal".
fact: Sub means under, and optimal means best, so suboptimal means under the best.
exercise: The report said the plan was «suboptimal». What does that mean in plain words? | *Not as good as it could be, or poor | Very loud | Brand new | Perfect and finished
exercise: In maths, «suboptimal» means what? | Cannot be used in school | *Works, but is not the best possible way | Gives the wrong answer every time | Is always the fastest way
exercise: Copy the word that means poor or bad in «The result was suboptimal, so we will try again.» | try | again | result | *suboptimal
test: A mum says the cake was «suboptimal» after it burned. What does she mean? | *It was burnt and not good | It was the best cake ever | It was made too early | It was made with chocolate
test: Why might someone say «suboptimal» instead of «bad»? | Because suboptimal means perfect | Because it is a word only used for food | *To sound polite, or to soften bad news | Because it is the longest word they know
exam: «Suboptimal» is best described as | A word only used in maths | A word only used in sport | A word meaning the best possible | *A polite, jargon word for poor or bad, used in business, science and polite criticism
